WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:03.190
- So, you recently opened up
about having panic attacks.

00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:04.290
I didn't know that about you.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.570
- Yeah, I moved out at 15,

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:07.660
so I started having panic
attacks about that age.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:08.840
Didn't know what they were.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:10.420
And then when I was homeless,

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.430
they hit a whole new level.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:14.600
And so I started inventing
tools and techniques

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.610
to help me with them.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:17.140
Haven't had them in ages.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.120
And then suddenly when I was filming,

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:20.090
maybe it was "The Masked Singer,"

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:22.420
I had my first panic attack in ages.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:23.900
- Well, probably 'cause
you were in a costume

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:24.820
that you couldn't breathe in, maybe?

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.120
- Claustrophobic, yeah, I
was a little claustrophobic.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.230
- Yeah.
- So I started posting,

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.130
'cause we help kids with anxiety disorders

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:31.870
and those types of things

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:33.272
and so started posting more about it

00:00:33.272 --> 00:00:34.105
(audience applauds)

00:00:34.105 --> 00:00:35.557
just to help people
understand what they are

00:00:35.557 --> 00:00:37.310
and the things you can do to help.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:38.510
- I had no idea what they were

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:39.770
until the first time I was pregnant.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:41.370
And I had one while driving.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:42.203
- Oh god, that's the worst.

00:00:42.203 --> 00:00:43.036
- And I was terrified.

00:00:43.036 --> 00:00:45.210
It was like, I froze and
I'd never had one before,

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.490
but for some reason, I don't know,

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.070
chemically something
happened and I had one.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:50.860
I was like, "This is no good."

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:52.070
Like you freak out.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:53.560
- Your brain literally goes offline,

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.650
so all the blood drains
out of your frontal lobes

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.170
and goes right to your amygdala.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:58.590
And your vascular system changes,

00:00:58.590 --> 00:00:59.940
your blood pressure changes.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.830
- What's up, Bill Nye?
- Finding things to go-

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:05.352
- Geez. I was like,
"Okay, that's amazing."

00:01:05.352 --> 00:01:06.350
- Well, if you find things

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.450
that force the blood back
into your processing centers.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.830
So, smell, taste, touch.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:13.830
That forces your brain to
deal with the information,

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.317
so it forces it to go back online.

00:01:15.317 --> 00:01:17.696
- Forces it to come back.
(audience applauds)

00:01:17.696 --> 00:01:19.469
- Yeah.
- What in the world.

00:01:19.469 --> 00:01:22.180
Have you guys ever suffered
from any kind of panic attack

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:23.340
or any kind of?

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:24.640
Maybe like on set or something?

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:25.473
Do you all ever get like that?

00:01:25.473 --> 00:01:27.680
Like I would get terrified
of what y'all do,.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.040
- But I think it's so validating

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.040
hearing the kind of scientific side of it,

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.920
to hear that it is a
chemical reaction happening

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.320
and something that needs to
be addressed in that way.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.693
- Yeah, that reasoning part of your brain

00:01:38.693 --> 00:01:39.526
literally goes offline.

00:01:39.526 --> 00:01:40.359
There's no blood there.

00:01:40.359 --> 00:01:41.192
It's fascinating.

00:01:41.192 --> 00:01:42.639
- And that's the panic.

00:01:42.639 --> 00:01:44.810
Have you ever had anything like that?

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.367
- Of course, yeah, absolutely.

00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:48.920
Just something that just puts you

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.930
in your place completely
and you can't move.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.040
- Yeah, and it's your
brain noticing patterns.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.972
It only takes two data points

00:01:54.972 --> 00:01:58.120
that your brain, even your
subconscious recognizes,

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.810
to some other time that might
have been traumatic for you.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:02.470
And so its, I don't know-

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:03.450
It took me a long time to figure out

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.834
like what were those two things. You know,

00:02:05.834 --> 00:02:06.770
so then I could start creating a plan.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:09.270
- Your body keeps score, cause
you go back to that trauma

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.030
and you don't realize
but your brain does that.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.920
It remembers it.
- Yeah, its the pattern.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.770
It's fascinating.
- It's exhausting.

